      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Spinal subdural hematoma (SSH) is an uncommon entity. There is a higher incidence among patients with a bleeding diathesis and among those with a bleeding diathesis having a lumbar puncture. The case reported here is of a 65-year-old woman on oral anticoagulants presenting with atypical symptoms who developed SSH over a six-hour period. Emergency investigation and treatment are required because recovery is inversely related to the length of time of spinal cord compression. A review of the literature revealed 37 previously reported cases of nonmajor traumatic SSH."},
